  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Tirumala Ramesh vs The State Of Telangana on 13 June, 2025 Author: N.Tukaramji Bench: N.Tukaramji The Honourable Sri Justice N.Tukaramji Criminal Petition No. 6981 of 2025 Order",
    "facts": "This Criminal Petition is filed under Section 528 of Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 (for short 'BNSS, 2023') seeking quashment of the proceedings, against the petitioner in Crime No.238 of 2025 of Matwada Police Station, Warangal District. 2. The petitioner is sole accused in Crime No.238 of 2025 registered for the offences punishable under Sections 272 and 275 of Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 (for short 'BNS, 2023'). 3. 4. The prosecution case, in brief, is that the petitioner/accused is involved in selling and purchasing the banned Tobacco products and the Police concerned seized the said Tobacco products worth of Rs.4,64,000/-. 8. As per the prosecution, on 01.06.2025 at 17:50 hours, the complainant/ Sub Inspector of Police lodged a complaint alleging that the petitioner was found in possession of a large quantity of Government banned tobacco products. Accordingly, the complainant and his staff apprehended the petitioner and seized tobacco products viz., 1) M. Gold Packets: 160 x Rs.1000= Rs.1,60,000/-, 2) Mirage packets: 35xRs.500= Rs.17,500/-, 3) J.K. 3320 packets: Tirumala Ramesh vs The State Of Telangana on 13 June, 2025 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/74772818/ 1 110xRs.250=Rs.27,500/-, 4) Baba Black packets: 503 x Rs.300= 1,50,900/-, 5) VI packets:14 x Rs.50=Rs.700/-, 6) SSI packets: 18 x Rs.100=Rs.1,800/-, 7) K.P.Double Black packets: 7 x Rs.100/-, 8) ZL01 packets: 6 x Rs.100 = 600/-, 9) Maximus packets: 210 x Rs. 10= Rs.2,100/-, 10) ZX packets: 16 x Rs.100 = Rs.1,600/-, 11) M. Gold 3 NTR,J packets: 6 x Rs.100 = Rs.600/- and 12) Anarpackets: 200 x Rs.500 = Rs.1,00,000/-, which were total worth of Rs.4,64,000/- under cover of panchanama. 9. The related Notification No.501/FSS/I/2022 dated 07.01.2022,prohibiting manufacturing, storing, distribution, transportation and sale of gutka and pan masala in the entire State of Telangana,has been stayed by the Hon'ble Supreme Court of India in Shri Venkateshwara Traders v. State of Telangana and othersvideSLA No.20441 of 2021 with SLP(C) No.1656 of 2022. Thereafter, the Director General of Police, Telangana State issued Circular Memorandum in RC.No.295/R1-NS/2022 with directions to the Station House Officers not to interfere with the business activities, who carry out the business as per rules of Food Safety and Standards Authority of India and also not to take any coercive measures against the tobacco traders as long as the order of the Hon'ble Supreme Court is in force.",
    "arguments": "5. Learned counsel for the petitioner would submit that the Coordinate Bench of this Court vide Criminal Petition Nos.3731 and Batch [LEAKAGE_MASK] the proceedings against the petitioners therein by 2 NTR,J passing common order, dated 27.08.2018. He further submits that the 2nd respondent has registered the present crime in violation of the orders passed by this Court. Hence, prayed for quashment of the proceedings against the petitioner in the crime. 6. Learned Additional Public Prosecutor would submit that the petitioner has to face either investigation or trial and prove his innocence and instead of doing so, he has filed the present criminal petition, which cannot be considered at this stage. 10. Having regard to this factual position, a Coordinate Bench of this Court in Crl.P.No.6235 of 2022 held that against the allegations of illegal selling and transportation of tobacco products the Sections 188, 269, 270, 272, 273, 278, 328 read with Sections 511, 336, 409 and 420 4 NTR,J of I.P.C.,would not attract andquashed the proceedings against the petitioners therein.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "",
    "respondent_arguments": "5. Learned counsel for the petitioner would submit that the Coordinate Bench of this Court vide Criminal Petition Nos.3731 and Batch [LEAKAGE_MASK] the proceedings against the petitioners therein by 2 NTR,J passing common order, dated 27.08.2018. He further submits that the 2nd respondent has registered the present crime in violation of the orders passed by this Court. Hence, prayed for quashment of the proceedings against the petitioner in the crime. 6. Learned Additional Public Prosecutor would submit that the petitioner has to face either investigation or trial and prove his innocence and instead of doing so, he has filed the present criminal petition, which cannot be considered at this stage.",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "10. Having regard to this factual position, a Coordinate Bench of this Court in Crl.P.No.6235 of 2022 held that against the allegations of illegal selling and transportation of tobacco products the Sections 188, 269, 270, 272, 273, 278, 328 read with Sections 511, 336, 409 and 420 4 NTR,J of I.P.C.,would not attract andquashed the proceedings against the petitioners therein."
  },
